VALUES ('f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 'aws-saa-practice-3', 'AWS SAA-C03 Practice Paper 3', 'aws-saa', ARRAY['STS', 'SCPs', 'CloudHSM', 'Pre-signed URLs', 'Aurora', 'DynamoDB Global Tables', 'Snowball', 'Lambda Concurrency', 'Consolidated Billing']::TEXT[], 20, 'standard', true, 3, 30);

-- ── Domain: Design Secure Architectures (30% — 6 questions) ──

VALUES ('3b49753d-485a-4145-8a1f-0e82f8276862', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 1, 'An application running in Account A needs to temporarily access an S3 bucket in Account B, without creating a long-term IAM user or sharing credentials between accounts. What is the correct mechanism?');
VALUES ('3b49753d-485a-4145-8a1f-0e82f8276862', '9a21d63c-3351-4513-973d-3d03689956f0', 'STS AssumeRole is the standard, secure cross-account access pattern: Account B defines an IAM role with a trust policy specifying that Account A (or a specific principal within it) is allowed to assume it, and with a permissions policy scoping exactly what that role can do (e.g., read this specific S3 bucket). Account A''s application calls AssumeRole, receiving short-lived, automatically-expiring temporary credentials (typically 15 minutes to a few hours) — no long-term secret is ever shared between accounts, and the temporary credentials are scoped precisely to what''s needed. Duplicating IAM users/keys across accounts is exactly the anti-pattern this solves — long-term shared credentials are a real security liability (harder to rotate, easy to leak, no automatic expiration). Making a bucket fully public is a drastically over-broad, insecure solution to what should be a scoped, temporary need.');

VALUES ('fdeab22e-34b9-437b-a49d-4c1ef1230fe8', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 2, 'A company with dozens of AWS accounts under AWS Organizations wants to enforce an account-wide guardrail that NO account, regardless of any IAM permissions granted within it, can ever disable CloudTrail logging or leave a specific AWS Region. What AWS Organizations feature enforces this?');
VALUES ('fdeab22e-34b9-437b-a49d-4c1ef1230fe8', 'c8630595-6be9-4b9b-aceb-dedef18eec75', 'SCPs are specifically designed as an organization-wide permission CEILING — they don''t grant permissions themselves, but they define the maximum possible permissions for every account, user, and role beneath them in the OU/Organization hierarchy. Critically, an SCP restriction (e.g., "deny cloudtrail:StopLogging" or "deny actions outside these Regions") cannot be overridden by any IAM policy inside a member account, including that account''s own root/administrator user — this is exactly what makes SCPs the correct tool for a TRUE account-wide, non-overridable guardrail, unlike individual IAM policies (which apply per-user/role and CAN be modified by an account administrator). S3 bucket policies scope only to specific buckets, not account-wide API actions like CloudTrail management. Security Groups control network traffic (IP/port level), not IAM/API-level actions like disabling a logging service — a common category-confusion trap.');

VALUES ('20c1b3d9-3fd1-43d4-b392-9cc114e1cbbb', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 3, 'A financial services company has a strict regulatory requirement to use single-tenant hardware security modules (HSMs) that they have direct administrative control over for cryptographic key storage, rather than a shared/multi-tenant managed key service. Which AWS service meets this specific requirement, and how does it differ from KMS?');
VALUES ('20c1b3d9-3fd1-43d4-b392-9cc114e1cbbb', '7bbb1b71-2109-4596-9bbb-966abdc17130', 'CloudHSM provisions dedicated, single-tenant hardware security modules within a VPC that the customer directly administers (including managing the HSM cluster, generating/backing up keys, and full administrative control) — matching a strict regulatory requirement for dedicated, customer-controlled hardware. KMS, by contrast, is a fully-managed, shared multi-tenant service — AWS manages the underlying HSM infrastructure, and while KMS is FIPS 140-2 validated and highly secure, the customer does not get direct administrative control over dedicated hardware the way CloudHSM provides — this is a genuine, testable architectural distinction, not interchangeable branding. Macie is a data classification/PII-discovery service (scanning S3 for sensitive data), entirely unrelated to HSM/key storage.');

VALUES ('73cb13b4-6857-483a-8e44-42fa8de966ff', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 4, 'A three-tier application has a web tier and a database tier in separate subnets. The security architect wants the database tier''s Security Group to allow inbound traffic ONLY from instances in the web tier, regardless of the web tier''s IP addresses changing over time (e.g., due to Auto Scaling replacing instances). What is the correct Security Group configuration approach?');
VALUES ('73cb13b4-6857-483a-8e44-42fa8de966ff', 'e34db6f1-db8d-4dd8-aade-ed64a40e3079', 'Security Groups support referencing ANOTHER Security Group ID as the source (or destination) in a rule, instead of a CIDR block — this means "allow traffic from any instance that is currently a member of Security Group X," which automatically stays correct as instances in that group are added or removed (exactly what happens continuously with an Auto Scaling Group), with zero manual IP-list maintenance required. Hardcoding IPs is fragile and breaks the moment Auto Scaling replaces an instance with a new IP. Allowing 0.0.0.0/0 (the entire internet) on a DATABASE tier''s Security Group is a serious over-permissive security mistake, not a legitimate simplification. Security-Group-referencing-Security-Group is a real, commonly used, and frequently tested SAA feature — the fourth option is simply false.');

VALUES ('d34b1f95-7f45-4e0c-be92-b828e4730d6b', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 5, 'A media company wants to let anonymous users download a specific private S3 object for exactly 15 minutes (e.g., a link shared for a limited-time promotion), without making the object or bucket publicly accessible, and without requiring the user to have an AWS account or IAM credentials. What S3 feature enables this?');
VALUES ('d34b1f95-7f45-4e0c-be92-b828e4730d6b', '1b40cb8f-ebf5-439f-ac6e-a3b9d9b27e4e', 'A pre-signed URL is generated (via the SDK/CLI) by someone who already has legitimate access to the object, embedding a cryptographic signature and an expiration time directly into the URL itself — anyone possessing that specific URL can perform the specified action (typically GET) until it expires, with the underlying object/bucket remaining otherwise private and no AWS credentials needed on the requester''s end at all. This is the standard, purpose-built mechanism for exactly this "temporary, credential-less access to an otherwise-private object" scenario. Making an object public and manually deleting it is operationally fragile (requires manual/scripted cleanup, and the object is genuinely public — discoverable/cacheable by anyone — during that window, not access-controlled by possession of a specific link). Creating disposable IAM users per anonymous downloader is a real anti-pattern (IAM users are not meant to be created/destroyed at this frequency or for anonymous, unauthenticated use cases) — pre-signed URLs exist specifically so this isn''t necessary.');

VALUES ('2857e42d-5223-4dad-8f27-c9777ed5ccf5', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 6, 'A compliance team needs continuous, automated detection of when any AWS resource''s configuration drifts from an approved baseline (e.g., an S3 bucket''s encryption setting gets disabled, or a Security Group rule is loosened), with a historical record of every configuration change over time. Which AWS service is purpose-built for this?');
VALUES ('2857e42d-5223-4dad-8f27-c9777ed5ccf5', 'adeab327-5e60-4d78-943a-f6cee3388adc', 'AWS Config is purpose-built specifically for this: it continuously records the configuration state of supported resources, can evaluate that state against Config Rules (either AWS-managed or custom, e.g., "S3 buckets must have encryption enabled") to flag non-compliant resources, and maintains a full historical timeline showing exactly when and how a resource''s configuration changed — directly matching "detect drift from baseline" and "historical record of every change." CloudTrail logs WHO made an API call and WHEN (the action taken), which is related but distinct from Config''s focus on the resulting resource STATE over time — CloudTrail alone doesn''t evaluate compliance rules or show a resource''s configuration history the way Config does. CloudWatch is a metrics/logging/alarming service focused on operational performance data, not configuration-state compliance tracking. Trusted Advisor gives point-in-time best-practice recommendations across cost/performance/security/fault-tolerance categories, not a continuous configuration-change history.');

-- ── Domain: Design Resilient Architectures (26% — 5 questions) ──

VALUES ('6dafa992-ac67-486c-82b4-da58f68f3ae3', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 7, 'A globally distributed application needs a NoSQL database where data written in one Region is automatically, asynchronously replicated to tables in several other Regions, allowing low-latency reads AND writes from users in each of those Regions against their local table. What DynamoDB feature provides this?');
VALUES ('6dafa992-ac67-486c-82b4-da58f68f3ae3', 'c1d213e3-3c1a-4cd6-9005-f60135eea5f9', 'DynamoDB Global Tables provide fully managed, multi-active (not just read replicas — every replica table accepts both reads AND writes) replication across chosen Regions, with automatic, asynchronous propagation of writes made in any Region to all other participating Regions'' tables — exactly matching "low-latency reads AND writes from users in each Region against their local table," a multi-active pattern distinct from a typical primary/read-replica setup where only one Region accepts writes. DAX is an in-memory caching layer for a SINGLE table''s read performance (covered in Paper 2), not a multi-Region replication feature. "DynamoDB Read Replicas" isn''t the correct DynamoDB feature name — Read Replicas is an RDS term for a different, single-direction (not multi-active) replication pattern; conflating the two services'' terminology is a common test trap.');

VALUES ('8e99a7ed-0d0d-48c2-ac2f-9712d86a1137', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 8, 'A company running a MySQL-compatible database on RDS wants significantly higher availability and faster failover than standard RDS Multi-AZ provides, along with storage that is automatically replicated six ways across three Availability Zones. Which AWS database service should they migrate to?');
VALUES ('8e99a7ed-0d0d-48c2-ac2f-9712d86a1137', '9e273bad-1501-4b46-aec7-d416519f793a', 'Aurora is AWS''s own MySQL- and PostgreSQL-compatible relational database, architecturally distinct from standard RDS: its storage layer automatically and continuously replicates data six ways across three Availability Zones, and Aurora typically fails over faster than standard RDS Multi-AZ (often under 30 seconds) because of this storage-level design, plus Aurora supports up to 15 low-latency read replicas that can also serve as failover targets — directly matching "higher availability and faster failover... storage automatically replicated six ways across three AZs." Redshift is a data warehouse for analytics/OLAP workloads (large-scale aggregate queries), not a drop-in replacement for transactional MySQL OLTP workloads. Neptune is a graph database (for highly connected data like social networks or recommendation engines), not MySQL-compatible at all — a clear category mismatch.');

VALUES ('4dae2f76-a32b-4c69-9171-20343ee48494', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 9, 'An Auto Scaling Group needs to run a cleanup script (e.g., deregistering the instance from an internal service registry, flushing logs to S3) on each instance BEFORE it is actually terminated during a scale-in event, rather than the instance being abruptly killed. What ASG feature enables this graceful termination behavior?');
VALUES ('4dae2f76-a32b-4c69-9171-20343ee48494', 'facf7d7b-d405-4bac-b0bb-d2eda56bd386', 'Lifecycle Hooks are specifically designed to pause an instance at a defined point in the scale-in (or scale-out) transition — for termination, an instance enters a "Terminating:Wait" state for a configurable timeout (up to 48 hours, with heartbeat extension), during which an event is published (to SNS/EventBridge) that can trigger a Lambda function or other automation to perform cleanup (deregistering from a service registry, flushing logs, gracefully draining connections) before the instance actually completes termination and moves to "Terminating:Proceed." Target Tracking Scaling Policies control WHEN scaling actions happen based on a metric target (e.g., keep average CPU at 50%), not what happens to an individual instance during termination. The health check grace period controls how long a NEWLY LAUNCHED instance is given before health checks start being enforced (avoiding premature termination during startup) — a different mechanism for a different point in an instance''s lifecycle, not scale-in cleanup.');

VALUES ('dda022fe-1f9d-489a-b98f-0376edb295e4', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 10, 'A company wants their RDS automated backups and manual snapshots to also be available in a second Region, so that if the primary Region has an extended outage, they can restore the database there. What is the correct approach?');
VALUES ('dda022fe-1f9d-489a-b98f-0376edb295e4', '6301bc1f-d513-4f0d-b13f-6aeb72a8d1d0', 'RDS snapshots are Regional resources by default — they are NOT automatically available in other Regions. To achieve cross-Region disaster recovery from snapshots, RDS provides a specific "copy snapshot" capability that lets you copy a manual or (via AWS Backup) automated snapshot to a different target Region, where it can later be used to restore a new database instance if the primary Region becomes unavailable — this must be explicitly configured/triggered, not automatic. Multi-AZ (covered in Paper 1) provides HA WITHIN a Region via a standby in a second AZ — it is entirely separate from, and does not by itself provide, cross-REGION snapshot copying or disaster recovery. Understanding that Multi-AZ solves AZ-level failure while cross-Region snapshot copy (or a separate cross-Region read replica) solves REGION-level failure is a key SAA distinction.');

VALUES ('1728c008-c932-428f-bb4c-4da7b041a659', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 19, 'An EC2 instance occasionally fails its underlying hardware (system) status check due to a physical host issue, requiring the instance to be moved to new hardware. The application cannot tolerate manual intervention delays for this specific failure type. What EC2/CloudWatch feature can automatically handle this without an Auto Scaling Group?');
VALUES ('1728c008-c932-428f-bb4c-4da7b041a659', 'fd55589b-a9df-4f9a-afef-51041ed6d4f7', 'EC2 distinguishes two status check types: instance status (issues at the OS/instance level, e.g., a kernel panic) and system status (issues with the underlying physical hardware/host). A CloudWatch alarm can be configured to trigger the "Recover" action specifically on a system status check failure — this automatically stops the instance, migrates it to new, healthy underlying hardware, and restarts it, all while PRESERVING the instance''s ID, private IP address, elastic IPs, and attached EBS volumes — no Auto Scaling Group is required for this specific self-healing behavior on a single, standalone instance. This is a real, standalone EC2/CloudWatch feature, not dependent on ASG membership, and does not require manual AWS Support intervention for this common failure mode. S3 Versioning is an object-storage feature entirely unrelated to EC2 instance hardware recovery.');

-- ── Domain: Design High-Performing Architectures (24% — 5 questions) ──

VALUES ('5398ac54-daad-4f85-99b3-8bfd894864e4', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 11, 'An application needs to upload a 20GB video file to S3. A single PUT request is slow and, if it fails partway through, requires restarting the ENTIRE upload from scratch. What S3 feature addresses both the speed and the restart-from-scratch problem?');
VALUES ('5398ac54-daad-4f85-99b3-8bfd894864e4', '7f2d041e-9cc8-4748-a9c9-0c36f06974fd', 'S3 Multipart Upload splits a large object into independent parts (as small as 5MB each, up to thousands of parts) that can be uploaded in parallel — directly improving throughput/speed — and critically, if a specific part fails partway through, only THAT part needs to be re-uploaded and retried, not the entire 20GB object from the beginning, which is the direct fix for the "restart from scratch" problem. AWS recommends Multipart Upload for any object over 100MB, and it''s required (not just recommended) above 5GB. Versioning tracks multiple versions of a whole object over time — an unrelated concept to how a single upload is split/parallelized. Object Lock provides WORM (write-once-read-many) compliance protection against deletion/overwrite — also unrelated to upload mechanics.');

VALUES ('17195de3-c16d-4f2d-bbe4-591cb6b216f6', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 12, 'A company needs to migrate 500 terabytes of on-premises data to S3. Their available internet bandwidth would take several months to transfer this volume, which is unacceptable. What AWS service is designed specifically for this kind of large-scale, offline data migration?');
VALUES ('17195de3-c16d-4f2d-bbe4-591cb6b216f6', '69debb72-adc1-4188-8269-090de70775b3', 'The AWS Snow Family (Snowball, Snowball Edge, and Snowmobile for truly massive exabyte-scale migrations) exists specifically for large-volume, offline data transfer where available network bandwidth would make an online transfer impractically slow: AWS ships a physical, ruggedized storage device to the customer, the customer loads it with data locally over their own LAN (fast), ships it back to AWS, and AWS imports the data directly into S3 — completely bypassing the customer''s internet bandwidth as the bottleneck for the bulk data movement. CloudFront is a content delivery CDN for serving content to end users, unrelated to bulk one-time migration. Direct Connect provides a dedicated network link with more consistent bandwidth/latency than internet transfer, but is still bounded by its provisioned bandwidth — it does not transfer 500TB "in under 24 hours regardless of speed," and for a large enough volume relative to available bandwidth, physical transfer via Snowball is still faster and is the AWS-recommended pattern specifically for this volume/bandwidth mismatch. Transfer Acceleration speeds up transfers over the existing internet path via CloudFront edge locations — it does not eliminate a fundamental bandwidth-vs-volume mismatch the way physical device transfer does.');

VALUES ('06dbde70-35f4-4b2f-bd59-6a9fe4a5cc70', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 13, 'A latency-sensitive Lambda function experiences noticeably slow response times specifically on its first invocation after a period of inactivity, but is fast on subsequent invocations shortly after. What is this phenomenon called, and what AWS feature directly addresses it?');
VALUES ('06dbde70-35f4-4b2f-bd59-6a9fe4a5cc70', 'a115b5ea-6b4e-40a5-93cd-215c22238498', 'A "cold start" is the well-known Lambda phenomenon where, after a period without invocations, Lambda has to initialize a brand-new execution environment (download code, start the runtime, run any initialization code) before it can process the request — adding noticeable latency to that first invocation specifically, while subsequent invocations reuse the already-warm environment and respond quickly. Provisioned Concurrency directly addresses this by keeping a specified number of execution environments pre-initialized and ready at all times, so even a request after a period of inactivity hits an already-warm environment. Cold starts affect Lambda functions across all supported languages to varying degrees (not exclusively Python), and are a genuinely real, addressable characteristic — not something with zero available mitigation. Lambda does not use EBS volumes for its own execution environment in the way this option implies; EBS is unrelated to cold-start latency.');

VALUES ('dfad9208-76d9-4335-b3c0-4bc4eb4a8008', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 14, 'A tightly-coupled, network-latency-sensitive HPC (High Performance Computing) workload requires the lowest possible inter-instance network latency and highest network throughput between EC2 instances, more important than spreading instances across multiple AZs for fault tolerance. What EC2 Placement Group strategy fits this requirement?');
VALUES ('dfad9208-76d9-4335-b3c0-4bc4eb4a8008', 'b1562aef-7a24-4c14-b57e-5b60e35a40a2', 'A Cluster placement group packs instances close together within a single Availability Zone (often on the same underlying hardware racks with high-bandwidth, low-latency networking between them) — specifically optimized for exactly this scenario: tightly-coupled HPC/big-data workloads where inter-instance network performance matters more than fault tolerance, since a single infrastructure failure could affect many instances in the cluster at once (an explicit, accepted trade-off for this use case). Spread placement groups do the OPPOSITE — they deliberately place each instance on distinct underlying hardware to MINIMIZE simultaneous failure risk (maximizing fault tolerance), which is the wrong trade-off for a workload prioritizing raw network performance over resilience. Partition placement groups divide instances into logical partitions across distinct hardware sets (common for large distributed systems like Hadoop/Cassandra, balancing some fault isolation with some grouping) — not specifically the lowest-latency option; Cluster is the correct answer for the priority stated in this scenario specifically.');

VALUES ('b5d1a76e-caff-4bad-80aa-45e3b2a08db5', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 20, 'An application stores large CSV files in S3 and needs to retrieve only a handful of specific columns and rows matching a filter condition from each file, currently downloading and parsing the ENTIRE file client-side just to discard most of the data. Which S3 feature reduces this waste by filtering server-side, before data is even transferred?');
VALUES ('b5d1a76e-caff-4bad-80aa-45e3b2a08db5', 'd163619b-7517-40d4-a99f-0510532f581f', 'S3 Select lets an application issue a SQL-like expression (SELECT specific columns WHERE a condition is met) against a CSV, JSON, or Parquet object stored in S3, with S3 itself performing the filtering server-side and returning ONLY the matching, requested data over the network — instead of transferring the entire object and discarding most of it client-side, which wastes both network bandwidth and client-side processing time. AWS has reported this can reduce data transfer and improve retrieval performance significantly for exactly this "small subset of a large object" access pattern. Transfer Acceleration speeds up transfer of the FULL object via CloudFront edge locations — it does not filter content at all, an entirely different mechanism. Object Lock is a WORM compliance/retention feature, unrelated to query filtering. S3 absolutely supports this server-side filtering capability — it is a real, purpose-built, commonly used feature specifically to avoid the "download everything, use very little" inefficiency described.');

-- ── Domain: Design Cost-Optimized Architectures (20% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('c54b366f-3fe0-48f0-93f2-025acb437180', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 15, 'A company has 12 separate AWS accounts across different departments, each individually paying On-Demand rates and separately negotiating volume discounts. What AWS Organizations feature allows them to combine usage across all accounts to reach volume pricing tiers faster and receive one consolidated invoice?');
VALUES ('c54b366f-3fe0-48f0-93f2-025acb437180', 'f1d520ef-e18a-4b18-95a2-695a58bc0a30', 'Consolidated Billing is a core AWS Organizations capability: a designated management/payer account receives one combined invoice covering every member account''s usage, AND usage is aggregated across all accounts for the purpose of reaching volume-based pricing tiers/discounts (e.g., S3 volume discount tiers, Reserved Instance/Savings Plan sharing across accounts) faster than any single account could reach alone — directly matching both stated goals (combine usage for pricing tiers, one consolidated invoice), while still providing itemized per-account breakdowns for cost allocation/chargeback purposes. SCPs (covered earlier in this paper) are a permissions guardrail mechanism, entirely unrelated to billing aggregation. Trusted Advisor provides best-practice recommendations across several categories, but does not itself combine or manage cross-account billing.');

VALUES ('64cb552f-70c9-40c2-bf54-0a292bbb70b0', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 16, 'A company hosts a public dataset in S3 that is downloaded frequently by external researchers, and wants those external downloaders to bear the data transfer cost instead of the company''s own AWS account absorbing it, while keeping the bucket accessible without requiring a support contract or complex access negotiation. What S3 feature enables this?');
VALUES ('64cb552f-70c9-40c2-bf54-0a292bbb70b0', '76150410-3ec0-48ff-b97f-65da644b09cf', 'S3 Requester Pays is specifically designed for exactly this scenario — a bucket owner hosting data that will be downloaded heavily by others (a public dataset, shared research data) can enable Requester Pays so that the downloading party''s AWS account is billed for the data transfer and request costs, while the bucket owner continues to pay only for storage — the requester must include specific authentication/headers acknowledging they''ll be charged, but no separate support contract or bespoke access negotiation is required beyond that. Object Lock is a WORM compliance/retention feature (preventing deletion/overwrite for a period), entirely unrelated to billing responsibility. Transfer Acceleration speeds up transfers via CloudFront edge locations but does not change WHO is billed — that remains the bucket owner unless Requester Pays is separately enabled.');

VALUES ('224c0faf-985a-4c18-9a92-1a6f267e1e33', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 17, 'A company''s traffic follows a highly predictable pattern: heavy load every weekday from 9 AM to 6 PM, and minimal load overnight and on weekends. They currently run a fixed-size fleet sized for peak load 24/7, which is expensive. What Auto Scaling approach would reduce cost while maintaining performance during known peak hours?');
VALUES ('224c0faf-985a-4c18-9a92-1a6f267e1e33', '27cddc52-6618-4d9a-a286-29632c6829fc', 'Scheduled Scaling is the correct fit specifically because the traffic pattern is KNOWN and PREDICTABLE (not reactive/unknown) — it lets an administrator define scaling actions at specific times (increase capacity right before the predictable weekday 9 AM ramp-up, decrease it after the predictable 6 PM drop-off), proactively matching capacity to demand rather than waiting for a dynamic scaling policy to react to a metric threshold being crossed after load has already increased. This directly reduces cost (fewer running instances overnight/weekends) while maintaining performance during the known peak window (capacity is already scaled up before peak arrives, not scrambling to catch up). Auto Scaling absolutely supports time-based scheduled actions — this is a real, commonly used, and frequently tested ASG feature, making manual daily instance management entirely unnecessary and strictly worse operationally. Simply shrinking the fleet permanently would cause real performance degradation during the (still real, still needing full capacity) weekday peak — not an acceptable trade-off when Scheduled Scaling solves the actual problem directly.');

VALUES ('d2f7b4be-fdc9-44d0-9f96-e5a32a61e818', 'f2826ae7-810c-46e3-ba03-0d1634bbe020', 18, 'A finance team wants visibility into which AWS service and which team is driving month-over-month cost increases, and wants to be automatically notified if spending is on pace to exceed a set monthly threshold before the month ends. Which two AWS services together address this?');
VALUES ('d2f7b4be-fdc9-44d0-9f96-e5a32a61e818', '2b261d71-f596-4401-97ee-68fe120f8ab7', 'Cost Explorer provides visual analysis of historical and current cost/usage data, filterable and groupable by service, linked account, tag, and other dimensions — directly answering "which service/team is driving cost increases." AWS Budgets lets you define a spend (or usage) threshold and set up alerts — critically, including alerts based on FORECASTED spend, meaning you can be notified that you''re on pace to exceed a monthly budget before the month actually ends and the overage has already happened, not just after the fact. Config and CloudTrail are configuration-compliance and API-activity-logging services respectively — genuinely useful tools, but not built for cost visibility or budget alerting. Inspector (vulnerability scanning) and GuardDuty (threat detection) are both security services, entirely unrelated to cost management — a clear category mismatch distractor.');
